<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp9sec2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Algebraic Properties of the Determinant</title>



    <p>
      We conceptualize the determinant as originating as a geometric object, measuring geometric properties of transformations. However, from this conceptualization emerges numerous valuable algebraic properties. In this lesson we will develop some of these algebraic properties.
    </p>
    <p>
      From observations in the previous sections on <m>\operatorname{vsf}</m> and <m>\sgn</m>, we get the following two propositions.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-elementarydet">
      <title>Elementary Matrix Rules</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>E</m> is an elementary matrix, then
        </p>
        <me>
          \det(E)=
          \begin{cases}
            -1 \amp \text{if } E \text{ is type 1,}\\
            k  \amp \text{if } E \text{ is type 2 with scaling factor } k\text{, and}\\
            1  \amp \text{if } E \text{ is type 3.}
          \end{cases}
        </me>
      </statement>
    </proposition>

    <remark>
      <p>
        Observe that the determinant of an elementary matrix is never zero.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <p>
      Since both <m>\operatorname{vsf}</m> and <m>\sgn</m> satisfy the product formula, so does <m>\det</m>!
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="thm-detproductformula">
      <title>Product Formula</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are <m>n\times n</m> matrices, then 
        </p>
        <me>
          \det (AB) = \det (A)\det(B).
        </me>
      </statement>
    </proposition>

    <p>
      Taking <xref ref="prop-elementarydet" /> and <xref ref="thm-detproductformula" /> as our core determinant properties, we may both rigorously deduce all other algebraic properties of the determinant and compute determinants.
    </p>

    <question>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute the determinant of the following <m>4\times 4</m> matrix:
        </p>
        <sage>
          <input>
            A0 = elementary_matrix(ZZ,4,row1=0, row2=3,scale=1)
            A1 = elementary_matrix(ZZ,4,row1=2,scale=2)
            A2 = elementary_matrix(ZZ,4,row1=1, row2=2,scale=1)
            A3 = elementary_matrix(ZZ,4,row1=1,row2=3)
            A4 = elementary_matrix(ZZ,4,row1=3,row2=0, scale=3)
            A = A0*A1*A2*A3*A4
            print(f"A = {A}")
          </input>
        </sage>
        <p>
          Use your result to describe how it transforms regions in <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </question>

    <p>
      More generally, we have the following strategy for computing determinants using elementary matrix decompositions.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-det_compute_with_emd">
      <title>Reading <m>\det</m> from an EMD</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is an <m>n\times n</m> matrix with elementary matrix decomposition <m>A=E_1\cdots E_r \operatorname{rref}(A)</m>,
          with <m>t_1</m> many type 1 elementary matrices, and <m>t_2</m> many type 2 elementary matrices with scaling factors <m>k_1,\ldots, k_{t_2}</m>,
          then 
        </p>
        <me>
          \det(A)=\begin{cases}0\amp \text{if } \operatorname{rref}(A)\ne I,\\ (-1)^{t_1}(k_1\cdots k_{t_2})\amp \text{if } \operatorname{rref}(A)=I.\end{cases}
        </me>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          If <m>\operatorname{rref}(A)\ne I</m>, then <m>\operatorname{rref}(A)</m>, and hence <m>A</m>, is a projection, so <m>A</m> has volume scaling factor 0.
          If <m>\operatorname{rref}(A)=I</m>, then <m>A</m> is a product of elementary matrices. 
          The computation follows from putting together <xref ref="prop-elementarydet" /> and <xref ref="thm-detproductformula" />.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

    <p>
      <xref ref="prop-det_compute_with_emd" /> is useful for computing the determinant of any matrix. 
      In the next section, I will discuss other strategies for computation.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-triangulardet">
      <title>Determinant of Triangular Matrices</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>T</m> is a lower (or upper) triangular matrix with diagonal entries <m>d_1,d_2, \ldots d_n</m>, then <m>\det(T)=d_1d_2\cdots d_n</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          The details of this proof I leave as a homework assignment. I will give a few hints.
        </p>
        <p>
          First, show that if none of the <m>d_i</m>'s are <m>0</m>, then there exists a sequence of type 3 row operations reducing <m>T</m> to the diagonal matrix <m>T_0</m> with diagonal entries <m>d_1,d_2, \ldots d_n</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Second, if one of the <m>d_i</m>'s is <m>0</m>, then <m>\det(T)=0</m>.
          Among other results, you may find <xref ref="prop-elementarydet" />, <xref ref="thm-detproductformula" />, as well as the big ideas of  <xref ref="chp8sec3" />, useful.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

    <p>
      Conceptually, the only class of matrix transformation not invertible are projections, which happen to smash volumes to zero. It turns out that the determinant is a useful criteria to distinguish projections from the invertible transformations.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-det_list">
      <title>The Determinant and Invertibility</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is an <m>n\times n</m> matrix, then the following two statements are equivalent:
        </p>
        <me>
          \text{A is invertible} \iff \det(A)\ne 0.
        </me>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          To show equivalence, I need to show each implies the other.
        </p>
        <p>
          To begin, write <m>A=E_1\cdots E_r \operatorname{rref}(A)</m> where <m>E_i</m> are elementary matrices.
          Since elementary matrices have nonzero determinant (<xref ref="prop-elementarydet" />), the product formula (<xref ref="thm-detproductformula" />) and the fact that the product of nonzero real numbers is nonzero implies that <m>c = \det(E_1\cdots E_r)</m> is a nonzero number.  
          Hence the product formula implies <m>\det(A) = c\det(\operatorname{rref}(A))</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is invertible, then <m>\operatorname{rref}(A)=I</m> (Algorithm <xref ref="algorithm-inverse" />) and we deduce <m>\det(A)=c\ne0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is not invertible, then <m>\operatorname{rref}(A)\ne I</m> is a projection, hence <m>\det(A) = c(0)=0</m>.
          Since a statement is logically equivalent to its contrapositive, we deduce that if <m>\det(A) \ne 0</m>, then <m>A</m> is invertible.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

    <p>
      <xref ref="prop-det_list" /> is an important computational check that is added to <em>The List</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We now discuss two useful algebraic properties of the determinant. They both have clear geometric interpretations and can be rigorously proven from above known propositions. Make sure you understand both!
    </p>

    <question>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give a geometric explanation as to why <m>\begin{bmatrix}2\amp 0\\0\amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{2}\amp 0\\0\amp \frac{1}{3}\end{bmatrix}</m> have reciprocal determinants.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </question>

    <p>
      If a matrix transformation <m>T</m> is invertible, then whatever <m>T</m> does to volumes and sign, its inverse <m>T^{-1}</m> should undo it.  
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-det_inverse">
      <title>Determinant of the Inverse</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is invertible, then <m>\det(A^{-1}) = \frac{1}{\det(A)}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Since <m>A</m> is invertible, it has an inverse <m>A^{-1}</m> which satisfies the equation <m>AA^{-1}=I</m> (Definition <xref ref="def-inverse" />).  
          Using the Product Formula (<xref ref="thm-detproductformula" />),
        </p>
        <me>
          \det(A)\det(A^{-1}) = \det(AA^{-1})=\det(I)=1.
        </me>
        <p>
          Then solve for <m>\det(A^{-1})</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-det_transpose">
      <title>Determinant of the Transpose</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is square, then <m>\det(A^{T}) = \det(A)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I will prove this assuming <m>A</m> has an <m>LU</m>-decomposition (which will generically be the case). I will leave the case in which it does not to you.  
          Write <m>A= LU</m>.   
          Using the Sock and Shoes property of transpose (Proposition ), <m>A^T=(LU)^T=U^TL^T</m>.  
          Observe that the transpose of a lower (resp. upper) triangular matrix is an upper (resp. lower) triangular matrix.   
          Furthermore, the diagonals of a triangular matrix and its transpose are the same.  
          Using <xref ref="prop-triangulardet" />, <m>\det(U)=\det(U^T)</m> and <m>\det(L)=\det(L^T)</m>.
          Using the Product Formula <xref ref="thm-detproductformula" /> and putting this all together:
        </p>
        <me>
          \det(A^T)=\det(U^TL^T)=\det(U^T)\det(L^T)= \det(U)\det(L)=\det(L)\det(U)=\det(LU)=\det(A).
        </me>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

    <question>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Revisit Example <xref ref="example_simmatrixdecomp1" /> and give a geometric explanation as to why <m>\begin{bmatrix}2.5\amp -0.5\\-0.5\amp 2.5\end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{bmatrix}2\amp 0\\0\amp 3\end{bmatrix}</m> have the same determinant.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </question>

    <p>
      Fixing a matrix <m>A</m>, it does not matter which region of <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> you are looking at, be it the unit square, a <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-unit square, or something else, the volume scaling factor, sign, and hence determinant are all the same.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-det_smd">
      <title>Determinants of Similar Matrices</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>\mathcal{B}</m> is a basis and <m>A=P_{\mathcal{B}}BP_{\mathcal{B}}^{-1}</m>, then <m>\det(A) = \det (B)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Using the Product Formula <xref ref="thm-detproductformula" /> and Proposition <xref ref="prop-det_inverse" />:
        </p>
        <me>
          \det(A) = \det(P_{\mathcal{B}}BP_{\mathcal{B}}^{-1}) = \det(P_{\mathcal{B}})\det(B)\det(P_{\mathcal{B}}^{-1}) = \det(B).
        </me>
      </proof>
    </proposition>


</section>